{"kind":"report","text":"Independent review of exact clean head a0e9454922b2934e5494eb11dde9cca8ce8b2d5f on request/kernel-race-aware-alloc-count, verified in my own detached clean checkout at exactly that commit; branch and origin both resolve to the named head, single commit cut from main 5ff5d4db6. The diff is exactly as reported: two complementary build-tagged files defining unexported raceEnabled (package kernel, no collisions anywhere), three allocation ceilings made conditional on !raceEnabled at checkpoint_cache_test.go 541/825/859, one explanatory comment, and no production change — checkpoint.go untouched. I did not take guard placement on trust: tightening the scratch ceiling from 8 to 7 makes the plain run fail with 'preflight refusal allocated 8 times' while the race run passes; breaking the in-closure refusal check makes BOTH plain and race runs fail at line 822 — so the count assertion is live only where intended and the refusal-behaviour assertions are live under both modes. Gates rerun by me at this exact tree: go vet ./internal/kernel/ clean; go test -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ ok; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/kernel/ ok — the race gate is green again. ARCHITECTURE: internal/kernel test contract only; docs/reference/architecture.md layer contracts are untouched because no production surface changes; a build-tag constant inside the test package is the right seam given the race-mode allocation count is unstable (8 alone, 9 in-package) and any race ceiling would pin the detector's bookkeeping rather than the code — skipping the count while keeping behaviour assertions is correct. SECURITY: no trust boundary, signature, secret or bound changes; production bounds are unchanged and fail-closed refusal behaviour remains asserted under both modes, probe-proven; the skipped ceilings are measurement assertions, not runtime limits. SIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut; I specifically rule that widening from planner request #10746's one test to three is IN scope — all three carry the identical latent defect, one had zero headroom (ceiling 1 against measured 1), and narrowing would leave the race gate red, defeating the request's own condition. ONE FOLLOW-UP TO WEIGH, not a defect of this head and pre-existing in spirit: for the scratch-preflight path the bounded writer refuses downstream anyway, so under -race — where the count is now skipped — nothing pins the preflight's existence itself; whether that deserves a child request for a race-stable structural pin is the requester's call. APPROVED.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:43b91ffb9d4bb61a0b1a8b6ab305843d96d6e63f","head":"a0e9454922b2934e5494eb11dde9cca8ce8b2d5f","verdict":"approved"}}